WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.200
"The Matrix Resurrections" is a movie about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:07.440
making "The Matrix Resurrections."&nbsp;
We cannot emphasize this enough.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:08.400
"Am I crazy?"

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:10.960
"We don't use that word in here."

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:15.440
The end of “Resurrections” begins with&nbsp;
a much-anticipated sit down between Neo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:20.240
and The Analyst. Their venue for this meetup is&nbsp;
the delightfully-named Simulatte coffee shop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:25.200
where Thomas Anderson has received much of his&nbsp;
stimulation over the years — and inspiration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:31.280
via a beautiful woman named “Tiffany” who bears&nbsp;
a striking resemblance to our old friend Trinity.

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:35.120
This "Tiffany" has a lot invested&nbsp;
in the world she sees every day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:40.240
She has a husband, a job, three children,&nbsp;
but also a persistent feeling there must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:44.160
be more – as well as a relentless&nbsp;
affection for Ducati motorcycles.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:49.200
At the coffee shop, Neo and the Analyst finally&nbsp;
lay all their cards on the table and decide to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:54.640
let her break the stalemate. If Tiffany decides to&nbsp;
stay Tiffany, Neo will return to the Matrix once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:59.840
and for all and become Thomas Anderson again; if&nbsp;
she decides to become Trinity, all bets are off.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:04.720
The Architect, who says he likes tests,&nbsp;
accepts, saying, "We’ll let Tiff decide.”

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:09.920
Breaking down the stakes, the grown-up Sati&nbsp;
sums up Neo's dilemma with a single line:

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:14.400
“The most important choice in&nbsp;
Neo’s life isn’t his to make.”

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:19.680
After watching Moss’s character essentially&nbsp;
being lobotomized for most of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:24.160
what comes next is particularly satisfying.&nbsp;
As her husband and kids make one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:27.840
last sales pitch, it appears that&nbsp;
Tiffany might return home with them;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:31.680
just as Neo is being dragged away,&nbsp;
however, she delivers four words that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:37.040
trigger the film’s ending — and perhaps, more&nbsp;
“Matrix” movies to come: “My name is Trinity.”

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:43.200
One of the more welcomed upgrades in “The Matrix&nbsp;
Resurrections” is the new Agent Smith. Although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.600
Hugo Weaving was nothing short of iconic&nbsp;
in his work originating the character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:50.480
Jonathan Groff does a fine job as his new face.

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:54.960
At the end of “Revolutions,” it looked as&nbsp;
if both Neo and Smith had been destroyed.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:02:00.080
With “Resurrections,” we once again see that&nbsp;
Smith and Neo’s fates are tied to each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:03.280
As his “boss” becomes his “enemy,” Groff’s Smith&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:08.480
takes over in the key Matrix role of&nbsp;
engaging Neo in multiple high-powered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:13.920
memorable smackdowns. By the end of the film,&nbsp;
it looks as if Smith might have been eradicated.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:19.760
But when Neo and Trinity need saving, Smith once&nbsp;
again returns from the dead, this time to fight on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:25.600
their side. He substantially aids their efforts&nbsp;
in the Simulatte coffee shop. Then, vanishing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:31.600
once again, he points out to our heroes, “Anyone&nbsp;
could be you. Whereas, I’ve always been anyone.”

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:38.240
What follows is all the butt-kicking, bullet&nbsp;
dodging, aerial acrobatic choreography&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:42.720
you would expect from a “Matrix” film,&nbsp;
this time with Neo and Trinity backed up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:47.520
by a new crew led by Bugs. As the group&nbsp;
make their way out of the Simulatte,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:52.640
the Analyst turns the Matrix against&nbsp;
these viruses threatening his creation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:58.720
In essence, every human in the area turns into a&nbsp;
quasi-zombie, attempting to take down the heroes.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:02.880
When Trinity shows up on a Ducati and&nbsp;
has Neo hop on board, this sequence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:08.720
gets particularly striking. As in, hundreds&nbsp;
of bodies striking the streets down below,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:12.400
diving out skyscraper windows and&nbsp;
essentially turning themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:16.720
into bombs attempting to hit Trinity and&nbsp;
Neo as they speed through the streets.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:22.160
After many close calls, explosions and imperiled&nbsp;
colleagues, Trinity and Neo make it to their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:28.000
destination. But what comes next? If people are&nbsp;
truly to be freed, the Analyst himself must be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:33.040
confronted. But with Neo and Trinity united again,&nbsp;
and their minds freed, anything is possible.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:38.240
With that bit of business concluded and&nbsp;
agents closing in, Neo and Trinity take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:42.160
to the roof of a skyscraper, where&nbsp;
they take a great leap of faith.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:47.440
Throughout “The Matrix Resurrections,” Neo&nbsp;
keeps seeing the same recurring image of birds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:52.960
in flight, flying in some sort of time loop.&nbsp;
We are also told that Thomas Anderson tried to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:58.000
step off the roof of a building at a corporate&nbsp;
party but was pulled back at the last moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:03.040
an event witnessed by Bugs. Finally, it&nbsp;
becomes a sort of running joke throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:08.080
“Resurrections” that people ask Neo whether he&nbsp;
can still fly – and he can’t make it happen.

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:12.400
All this builds to Neo and Trinity,&nbsp;
literally taking a leap of faith.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:17.200
Stepping off the top of the building, holding&nbsp;
hands, at first they begin to fall — and you’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:22.560
be forgiven if you just assume Neo’s old skills&nbsp;
will kick in and he’ll save the day. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:27.760
it’s Trinity who discovers an ability to fly.&nbsp;
Reminiscent of Neo soaring away at the end of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:33.360
the original “Matrix,” this time it’s Trinity who&nbsp;
takes to the sky, with Neo along for the ride.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:39.360
When the duo confront the Analyst together, this&nbsp;
time in his office, the power dynamic has shifted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:45.280
considerably. Now, instead of dictating the&nbsp;
terms to Neo, surrounded by dozens of his agents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:50.880
he is on his heels and alone. But that doesn’t&nbsp;
stop the Analyst from bragging about his plans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:54.800
waxing philosophic about the&nbsp;
intrinsic shortcomings of mankind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:58.080
and telling the duo time and again&nbsp;
that their efforts are useless.

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:01.840
As if to drive home the point that he’s&nbsp;
the one fighting the losing battle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:06.880
Trinity kills the Analyst — again and again,&nbsp;
each time with him snapping back until he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:11.920
finally seems to admit defeat. At this point,&nbsp;
the Analyst seems to shift from saying they’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:16.400
never gain control to essentially saying&nbsp;
they’ll hate the burden of having control.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:20.400
What are they going to do, he asks, rebuild&nbsp;
everything with rainbows in the skies?

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:23.280
As he puts it, “The sheeple&nbsp;
aren’t going anywhere."

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:28.000
At this point,the newly-reunited Trinity&nbsp;
and Neo resolve to do exactly that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:31.040
joking that the rainbow idea&nbsp;
actually sounds pretty nice.

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:33.680
If you stick around through the credits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:37.840
you’ll notice that when the title “The Matrix&nbsp;
Resurrections” is flashed on the screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:43.360
it becomes “The Matrix Resurrects.” Could&nbsp;
this be the title of the next potential film?

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:46.560
Like the end of the original film,&nbsp;
the final moments are soundtracked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:50.400
by the uplifting rebellion rock&nbsp;
of “Wake Up.” Only this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:53.520
rather than the ferocious Rage&nbsp;
Against the Machine original,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:59.200
the cover version you hear is by a band called&nbsp;
Brass Against, with help from Sophia Urista.

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:03.440
Unfortunately, the post-credits scene is a&nbsp;
real disappointment. When the titles lift and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:08.320
the screen lights up again, we are transported&nbsp;
back to Thomas Anderson’s old place of work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:12.320
where his team are still debating new&nbsp;
marketing ideas and ways to make money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:16.480
off the “Matrix” franchise. As in the&nbsp;
earlier scenes, it’s all very meta.

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:21.840
Someone says, “Face reality: movies are&nbsp;
dead, games are dead. Narrative is dead.”

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:26.800
Their solution? Create programming for felines:&nbsp;
a series of videos called the Cat-trix.

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:31.040
And, yeah, that's real. "The Matrix&nbsp;
Resurrections" is in theaters now.

